TryHackMe: Introduction to SIEM
Platform: TryHackMe
Room: Introduction to SIEM
Difficulty: Easy
Date: 2026
Objective
Learn the fundamentals of Security Information and Event Management (SIEM), including how SIEM platforms collect, analyze, and correlate logs to support threat detection, incident response, and security monitoring in a SOC environment.

Step 1: Understanding SIEM
The room introduced SIEM as a centralized security solution used to collect and analyze logs from multiple systems and devices.
Main SIEM functions:


Log collection


Event correlation


Threat detection


Security monitoring


Incident investigation


Alert generation


SIEM platforms help security teams detect suspicious activity across enterprise environments in real time.

Step 2: Why SIEM Is Important
Organizations generate massive amounts of log data from:


Firewalls


Servers


Endpoints


Applications


Authentication systems


Network devices


Without SIEM, analyzing this data manually would be extremely difficult.
Benefits of SIEM:


Centralized visibility


Faster threat detection


Improved incident response


Compliance monitoring


Automated alerting


SIEM solutions are a core component of modern SOC operations.

Step 3: Log Sources and Data Collection
The room explained how SIEM systems ingest logs from different sources.
Common log sources:


Windows Event Logs


Linux system logs


Firewall logs


IDS/IPS alerts


Web server logs


Antivirus and EDR alerts


SIEM platforms normalize and organize this data so analysts can investigate events more efficiently.

Step 4: Correlation and Detection
One of the main features of SIEM is event correlation.
Correlation allows SIEM platforms to:


Link related events together


Detect suspicious patterns


Identify potential attacks


Reduce false positives


Example detections:


Multiple failed login attempts


Privilege escalation activity


Malware alerts


Unusual network traffic


Brute-force attacks


Correlation rules help analysts identify threats that may not be visible from a single log source.

Step 5: SIEM Components
The room introduced common SIEM architecture components.
Core components:
Log Collection
Agents or collectors gather logs from endpoints and devices.
Data Normalization
Logs are converted into a standardized format.
Correlation Engine
The SIEM analyzes events and applies detection rules.
Dashboards and Visualization
Security analysts use dashboards to monitor activity and investigate alerts.
Alerting System
Suspicious activity generates alerts for SOC analysts.

Step 6: SIEM Use Cases
The room covered several real-world SIEM applications.
Common use cases:


Threat detection


User activity monitoring


Compliance auditing


Insider threat detection


Incident response


Network monitoring


SIEM platforms provide analysts with visibility across the entire infrastructure.

